[LEHR.FullName]
Battle for Normandy

[LEHR.Epilogue]
Over the next two weeks, the division fought a defensive battle of attrition. On 19 July, Saint-Lô fell to the Americans. Six days later, the Americans launched Operation Cobra, their breakout from the Normandy lodgment. By that time, the division had only 2,200 combat troops remaining and 12 Panzer IV and 16 Panthers fit for action and 30 tanks in various states of repair behind the lines. The operation was preceded by a massive aerial bombardment by over 1,500 allied bombers. Panzer Lehr was directly in the path of attack and the division suffered about 1,000 casualties during this bombardment. The division also lost at least 14 assault guns and 10 tanks. Despite strong initial resistance, by 27 July the German defenses has been penetrated. On the same day, Bayerlein reported that Panzer Lehr was "finally annihilated."

On 1 August, the Panzer Lehr had 33 tanks and assault guns operational and a further 44 in workshops. and so on August 17 after a fighting withdrawal, it was ordered back to Alençon for rest and refitting. The division was subsequently called back to Germany for rest and refitting. During August, the division suffered 1,468 casualties.

Within seven months of its formation, the division was reduced to a combat-ineffective unit with only 20 remaining tanks. At one point, in September, it consisted only of a panzer grenadier battalion of company strength, an engineer company, six 105mm howitzers, five tanks, a reconnaissance platoon, and an Alarmbataillon (emergency alert battalion) of about 200 men recruited from stragglers and soldiers on furlough in Trier. After spending a month refitting in the Saar, the division was moved to Paderborn, receiving 72 tanks, 21 assault guns and replacements, to compensate for the losses suffered in Normandy.

[LEHR.Description]
Early on the morning of June 6th, 1944 the Allied landings at Normandy began. After a bitter struggle to secure the beachheads, Allied war plans began to focus on their first main strategic goal, the city of Caen. Due to errors in intelligence and Hitler's own refusal to commit sufficient forces early on, only the Wehrmacht 21st Armored Division was prepared to conduct any form of active resistance. By mid June however, several additional German units had arrived in Normandy but arrived too late to keep the Allied from heavily fortifying their beachheads. These new units included the 2nd Panzer Division, the 1st, 9th, 10th and 12th SS Hitlerjugend Division, plus the 17th SS Panzergrenadier Division and lastly the elite Panzer Lehr Division. Against them were assembled no fewer than 28 American, Canadian and English combat Divisions as well as 5 Armored Brigades. The stage was set for the vicious Battle for Normandy. For Germany, it was their best and only chance to push the Allies back into the sea. For the Allied forces, the campaign would ultimately seal the fate for all of Europe!
[L001.FullName]
Mousetrap
[L001.Description]
Early in the morning, the British 7th Armoured Division (the Desert Rats) tried to outflank Panzer Lehr and capture Villers-Bocage. Supported by overwhelming airpower, the British launched a multi-pronged attack.
[L002.FullName]
No way back
[L002.Description]
Having accomplished a 90-mile march to the front line, the Panzer Lehr Division took up positions to the South-East of Caen and to the left of the 12th SS Panzer Division Hitlerjugend. Three Panzer Divisions - 12th SS, 21st and Panzer Lehr - formed the main defensive shield around Caen. On June 14, the 50th division of the British XXX Corps launched a new attack against Panzer Lehr's 901st Panzergrenadier Regiment, defending the town of Tilly.
[L003.FullName]
Pre-emptive strike
[L003.Description]
During the entire Normandy campaign the Allied forces were able to call upon a seemingly endless supply of reinforcements and replacements. Meanwhile, the units belonging to Panzer Lehr suffered each day from constant aerial harassment and frequent ground combat losses. By June 15th, the British Army, advancing over a wide front, had managed to capture the village of Otto. Further advances threatened to cut off the 1st Battalion, 902nd Panzergrenadier. Understanding that, the commander of Panzer Lehr, General Bayerlein, ordered the re-capture of Otto.
[L004.FullName]
In defense
[L004.Description]
Throughout the middle of June, British units assaulted Panzer Lehr but were unable to completely dislodge this stubborn force.
[L005.FullName]
Firing line
[L005.Description]
In late June, the British 49th Infantry Division with support from the 8th Armoured, launched an offensive to capture the heights overlooking Rouray and further advance onward to Noyers-Bocage and Tournay-sur-Odon. Against this assault stood only 2 German Infantry Battalions and a few tanks from the 12th SS Hitlerjugend Division.
[L006.FullName]
Counter-attack
[L006.Description]
In early July, 1944 the Commander of Army Group West issued orders for a general counter-attack in Normandy where ever possible. Elements of  the 901st Panzergrenadier supported by heavy tanks were ordered to capture the small village to their front, which would help to destabilize the Allied positions.
[L007.FullName]
Steel frenzy
[L007.Description]
Panzer Lehr has arrived in the area of the Toit-Vire channel in order to strengthen the main battle line. Panzer Lehr wasted no time in launching an attack aimed at dislodging the enemy forces situated near the channel.
[L008.FullName]
Last battle
[L008.Description]
On July 25, 1944 the Americans launched Operation Cobra to push out westward from St. Lô. In the skies above, Allied bombers dumped over 3300 tones of bombs, rendering most German units below ineffective and shattered. Even with such a torrent above, Panzer Lehr would not give up without a fight.
